1
00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:03.370
In this lesson, you will learn what knowledge graphs are,

2
00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:05.672
various methods to construct them,

3
00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:10.877
and how they enable AI agents with better API discovery and execution

4
00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:13.480
in the context of enterprise applications.

5
00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.882
Let's dive in.

6
00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:18.051
In order to see how knowledge graphs are used in practice,

7
00:00:18.351 --> 00:00:22.188
let's do a quick web search. Let's search for SAP.

8
00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:25.792
What do you see as part of the result?

9
00:00:25.992 --> 00:00:28.361
Besides the list of matching websites,

10
00:00:28.361 --> 00:00:30.663
You'll see a comprehensive overview

11
00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:33.466
of this entity SAP drawn from a knowledge graph.

12
00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:36.269
Here you will see properties such

13
00:00:36.269 --> 00:00:38.571
as the description, link to a website,

14
00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:42.175
also relations to other entities such as the headquarters

15
00:00:42.175 --> 00:00:43.777
being Walldorf.

16
00:00:44.277 --> 00:00:46.379
Clicking on Walldorf leads you to

17
00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:48.782
the corresponding knowledge graph entry for Walldorf,

18
00:00:49.182 --> 00:00:51.384
where again you will see a list of properties

19
00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:55.155
such as description and relations to other entities,

20
00:00:55.155 --> 00:00:58.758
for example, the region that it's related to, Karlsruhe.

21
00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:02.095
Now that you've seen how knowledge graphs are used in practice,

22
00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:05.799
let's dive a bit deeper to understand their technical foundations.

23
00:01:06.199 --> 00:01:10.703
A knowledge graph represents structured information in the form of a graph

24
00:01:10.904 --> 00:01:13.206
where nodes represent entities

25
00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:17.077
and edges represent relationships between these entities.

26
00:01:17.377 --> 00:01:19.879
There are two main components of knowledge graphs.

27
00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.382
First, the schema or ontology,

28
00:01:22.682 --> 00:01:25.985
which defines the types of entities, their attributes,

29
00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:28.988
and relationships between the entities which occur in the knowledge graph.

30
00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:32.492
Second, the instance data,

31
00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:34.060
which are the instances that form the

32
00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:35.862
graph and follow the definitions of the schema.

33
00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:39.866
In general, there are two main approaches to represent knowledge graphs.

34
00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:43.970
labeled property graphs and the Resource Description Framework or RDF.

35
00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.973
While both of them model entities and relationships,

36
00:01:47.373 --> 00:01:51.978
they differ in their structure, expressiveness, and intended use cases.

37
00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:55.381
In this course, we will use RDF

38
00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:58.685
as a graph-based data model to represent knowledge graphs.

39
00:01:59.152 --> 00:02:01.454
The Resource Description Framework

40
00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:03.556
is a graph-based data model

41
00:02:03.556 --> 00:02:07.961
standardized by the Worldwide Web Consortium, in short, the W3C.

42
00:02:08.661 --> 00:02:10.363
RDF does not enforce a schema,

43
00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:13.166
which makes it flexible and easy to integrate data

44
00:02:13.166 --> 00:02:15.168
from heterogeneous data sources.

45
00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:18.972
SPARQL is the standard query language for RDF

46
00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:23.343
with well-defined semantics, similar to SQL for the relational data model.

47
00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:26.646
The atomic building block of RDF are triples.

48
00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:31.351
A triple consists of a subject, a predicate, and an object.

49
00:02:31.518 --> 00:02:34.420
Each triple can be represented

50
00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:37.524
as labeled directed edges in an RDF graph.

51
00:02:37.824 --> 00:02:41.327
For instance, the shown edges represent the fact

52
00:02:41.327 --> 00:02:43.530
that SAP is located in Walldorf.

53
00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:47.333
Now that you understand the fundamental elements of RDF graphs,

54
00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:49.536
let's take a look at an example knowledge graph

55
00:02:49.536 --> 00:02:52.338
similar to the one which was shown in our web search

56
00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:54.340
experiment previously.

57
00:02:54.741 --> 00:02:57.977
We first start with a node that represents SAP.

58
00:02:58.645 --> 00:03:02.115
In RDF, unique resource identifiers or URIs are used

59
00:03:02.115 --> 00:03:05.218
to uniquely identify resources in the graph.

60
00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:08.421
We can add attributes to the SAP instance,

61
00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:11.624
such as the year when it was founded, its name,

62
00:03:11.758 --> 00:03:13.960
and state that it's a company.

63
00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:17.931
Attribute values in RDF are so-called literal values.

64
00:03:18.331 --> 00:03:20.934
We can further expand the graph and link SAP

65
00:03:20.934 --> 00:03:23.136
to other nodes in the graph,

66
00:03:23.236 --> 00:03:25.538
such as Christian Klein as the CEO

67
00:03:25.738 --> 00:03:28.541
and Walldorf as a city of its headquarters location.

68
00:03:28.775 --> 00:03:31.945
One advantage of knowledge graphs is the ability to infer facts

69
00:03:31.945 --> 00:03:34.747
which are not explicitly stated in the data

70
00:03:34.747 --> 00:03:38.551
that can be derived from the definitions in the ontology.

71
00:03:38.751 --> 00:03:43.556
For instance, by defining located in as a transitive relationship,

72
00:03:43.556 --> 00:03:47.260
we can then infer that SAP is located in Germany.

73
00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:52.560
RDF graphs are usually serialized in a machine-readable format such as N-Triples

74
00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:55.268
or Turtle files as shown here.

75
00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:58.571
An example triple in this file indicates

76
00:03:58.571 --> 00:04:01.274
that SAP CEO is Christian Klein.

77
00:04:01.774 --> 00:04:04.043
There are various methods to construct knowledge graphs.

78
00:04:04.544 --> 00:04:08.047
You can use structured sources. Here you have a fixed schema

79
00:04:08.248 --> 00:04:10.650
where entities of the same type or class

80
00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:13.920
are described with the same attributes as in the case of relational

81
00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:17.724
databases. Here you will use mapping rules such as R2RML.

82
00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:20.226
You can also use semi-structured sources.

83
00:04:20.526 --> 00:04:23.730
Here you will have a flexible schema where entities

84
00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:25.632
of the same type or class

85
00:04:25.632 --> 00:04:27.734
may be described differently like XML documents.

86
00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:31.704
Here again, we use mapping rules to construct knowledge graphs.

87
00:04:32.071 --> 00:04:35.875
Of course, you could use Unstructured Sources in the knowledge graph.

88
00:04:35.875 --> 00:04:38.278
Here you do not have a fixed schema at all.

89
00:04:38.278 --> 00:04:41.281
For example, natural language text or images.

90
00:04:41.281 --> 00:04:43.416
NLP Techniques may be used to

91
00:04:43.416 --> 00:04:45.885
construct knowledge graphs from such data sources.

92
00:04:46.286 --> 00:04:50.089
In this course, you will work with Structured Sources

93
00:04:50.089 --> 00:04:52.292
to construct a sample knowledge graph.

94
00:04:52.992 --> 00:04:55.194
Here's a high level overview

95
00:04:55.194 --> 00:04:57.163
on the knowledge graph construction process.

96
00:04:57.563 --> 00:04:59.966
In step zero, you define the use case, the scope

97
00:04:59.966 --> 00:05:01.734
and the business questions that are

98
00:05:01.734 --> 00:05:03.770
needed to be answered by the graph,

99
00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:08.074
which triggers what metadata to extract, what to model, and so on.

100
00:05:08.675 --> 00:05:11.077
In step one, you begin by gathering data

101
00:05:11.077 --> 00:05:13.179
from across your enterprise ecosystem,

102
00:05:13.279 --> 00:05:15.882
which forms the foundation of the knowledge graph.

103
00:05:16.382 --> 00:05:19.185
The foundation stage is where you connect various systems

104
00:05:19.285 --> 00:05:21.387
and extract their valuable business data.

105
00:05:21.921 --> 00:05:25.925
Each source system speaks its own language and organizes data differently.

106
00:05:26.326 --> 00:05:29.729
For example, CSV, XML, JSON, and others.

107
00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:33.633
In step two, you analyze and standardize your data

108
00:05:33.633 --> 00:05:37.537
to ensure its knowledge graph ready. Think of it as

109
00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:40.373
translating different dialects into a common language,

110
00:05:40.773 --> 00:05:42.575
identifying duplicate information,

111
00:05:42.975 --> 00:05:46.179
resolving inconsistencies and validating data quality,

112
00:05:46.879 --> 00:05:49.382
all to ensure that knowledge graph is built

113
00:05:49.782 --> 00:05:52.685
on a solid foundation of trustworthy information.

114
00:05:53.319 --> 00:05:57.123
In step three, data modeling is where the magic happens.

115
00:05:57.623 --> 00:06:02.128
Here you convert the clean data into standardized RDF triples.

116
00:06:02.228 --> 00:06:07.228
Each piece of business information is represented as subject, predicate, object statements

117
00:06:08.101 --> 00:06:12.105
that directly mirror the relationships in the relevant underlying systems.

118
00:06:12.405 --> 00:06:14.307
And finally, in step four

119
00:06:14.407 --> 00:06:15.875
is where you leverage the connected

120
00:06:15.875 --> 00:06:17.610
nature of knowledge graphs to deliver value.

121
00:06:18.111 --> 00:06:20.613
These aren't just representations of the source systems.

122
00:06:21.247 --> 00:06:24.650
They're new connections, analysis, and insights

123
00:06:24.751 --> 00:06:28.855
derived from source metadata as an interconnected whole.

124
00:06:29.255 --> 00:06:31.357
Now that you know what a knowledge graph is

125
00:06:31.424 --> 00:06:33.326
and various methods to construct it,

126
00:06:33.426 --> 00:06:35.128
let's look at an example on how

127
00:06:35.128 --> 00:06:37.029
you can use knowledge graphs with AI agents

128
00:06:37.130 --> 00:06:38.831
in an enterprise context.

129
00:06:38.998 --> 00:06:41.200
Suppose you have a user request

130
00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:43.803
to create a purchase order for five pencils

131
00:06:43.803 --> 00:06:45.805
in purchasing group 002

132
00:06:45.805 --> 00:06:48.508
and purchasing organization 3000

133
00:06:48.508 --> 00:06:52.011
and want an AI agent to fulfill this request.

134
00:06:52.412 --> 00:06:55.515
Without any context, this is not a trivial task for the agent

135
00:06:55.882 --> 00:06:58.484
since the enterprise system landscape can be very complex

136
00:06:58.885 --> 00:07:01.888
with several thousand different APIs that might be relevant.

137
00:07:02.688 --> 00:07:05.491
Hence, we construct a knowledge graph of the APIs

138
00:07:05.892 --> 00:07:09.896
in the system landscape and enrich it with business process information

139
00:07:10.396 --> 00:07:12.098
to provide the right context to

140
00:07:12.098 --> 00:07:14.100
the agent to fulfill the user request.

141
00:07:14.567 --> 00:07:16.969
In large enterprise environments

142
00:07:16.969 --> 00:07:19.172
with complex evolving APIs,

143
00:07:19.172 --> 00:07:22.375
AI Agents face several challenges. Number one.

144
00:07:22.375 --> 00:07:25.378
LLMs often spend a lot of time and tokens

145
00:07:25.378 --> 00:07:27.380
just trying to discover the right API.

146
00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:29.382
It's a trial and error process

147
00:07:29.382 --> 00:07:31.384
that can slow things down significantly.

148
00:07:31.384 --> 00:07:33.553
Knowledge graphs help by organizing APIs

149
00:07:33.553 --> 00:07:35.555
around business processes and objects.

150
00:07:36.022 --> 00:07:39.225
This narrows down the search space, makes planning more efficient,

151
00:07:39.225 --> 00:07:41.527
reduces unnecessary replanning,

152
00:07:41.527 --> 00:07:45.131
and speeds up execution overall. Number two.

153
00:07:45.131 --> 00:07:47.934
While LLMs and retrieval-based systems like RAG

154
00:07:48.034 --> 00:07:50.136
are great at pulling relevant content,

155
00:07:50.436 --> 00:07:53.039
they often miss the deeper structure and context,

156
00:07:53.406 --> 00:07:57.610
especially when it comes to domain-specific logic or API metadata.

157
00:07:58.010 --> 00:08:00.313
Knowledge graphs bring that missing context.

158
00:08:00.813 --> 00:08:03.516
Because they are structured and semantically rich,

159
00:08:03.716 --> 00:08:05.918
they reflect the relationships between APIs,

160
00:08:05.918 --> 00:08:07.620
business processes and data models.

161
00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:11.224
This makes them especially helpful for grounding responses

162
00:08:11.524 --> 00:08:13.226
and avoiding hallucinations.

163
00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:16.662
particularly in dynamic or customized business environments.

164
00:08:16.863 --> 00:08:21.367
Number three, for simple read operations, LLMs usually cope well.

165
00:08:21.667 --> 00:08:24.070
But when tasks get more complex,

166
00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:27.373
like executing a series of API calls in the right order

167
00:08:27.673 --> 00:08:31.177
or understanding allowed fixed values, they start to struggle.

168
00:08:31.677 --> 00:08:36.677
Knowledge graphs offer a built-in structure that maps these dependencies and sequences,

169
00:08:37.183 --> 00:08:41.187
guiding the agents step-by-step and reducing the chances of error.

170
00:08:41.487 --> 00:08:45.424
And number four, AI agents typically generate answers

171
00:08:45.424 --> 00:08:47.627
based on learned patterns or retrieved snippets,

172
00:08:47.627 --> 00:08:50.429
but they rarely provide visibility into how they got there.

173
00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:52.832
That makes it hard to explain results

174
00:08:52.832 --> 00:08:54.734
or trace them back to source logic,

175
00:08:54.734 --> 00:08:58.237
especially when the same query returns different results.

176
00:08:58.704 --> 00:09:02.308
Knowledge graphs change that by making reasoning transparent.

177
00:09:02.909 --> 00:09:07.513
You can trace exactly which APIs were used with what filters,

178
00:09:07.713 --> 00:09:11.117
what the system returned, what was the source system, and so on.

179
00:09:11.717 --> 00:09:14.220
These are capabilities in enterprise settings

180
00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:18.991
where trust, auditability, and reproducibility are non-negotiable.
